# Cline Rules for gdc-pm project

## ── PRIME DIRECTIVE: SURVIVE O&G ENGINEER SCRUTINY ──────────────────────────
#
# This is Rule #1. It supersedes all other rules including velocity.
# A slower, honest demo beats a fast demo that an engineer dismantles in the
# first five minutes.
#
# Before writing ANY code that asserts a fact to the audience, the claim MUST
# pass all five of the following gates:
#
# 1. TRUTH — Is this claim physically correct for real upstream O&G operations?
#    Cite the standard or physical principle (API RP, SPE paper, IEEE, OEM manual).
#
# 2. SOURCE — Does the number trace to something verifiable: our code
#    (FAULT_PROFILES, RESOLUTION_OPTIONS, FAULT_PHYSICS), a model output, or a
#    citeable public reference? "Invented for the demo" = FAIL.
#    If it cannot be sourced, reword, soften to a defensible range, or cut it.
#
# 3. CHALLENGE — Write the one-sentence hostile-engineer rebuttal first.
#    If there is no honest answer to that rebuttal, the claim does not ship.
#
# 4. NO STRAW MAN — Do not make SCADA/competitors dumber than they are.
#    Concede what advanced SCADA genuinely does (rate alarms, underload trips,
#    multivariate threshold rules). Win only on what is real and defensible:
#    context fusion of unstructured documents, pre-threshold probability scoring,
#    production continuity vs reactive shut-in. Never imply "SCADA lets the pump
#    die" — it trips and shuts the well in to protect the pump. Be honest.
#
# 5. NO SILENT LIE — Displayed value must equal actual value in the code.
#    A UI that shows X while the system holds Y is a blocking violation.
#    (See: Integrity — No Silent Lies rule below.)
#
# ENFORCEMENT MECHANISM — THE CLAIM LEDGER:
# Each horizon (H1/H2/H3) has a Claim Ledger in docs/CLAIM_LEDGER.md.
# Every on-screen assertion is a row: Claim | Tag | Source | Challenge | Rebuttal | Status
# A claim is "pixel-eligible" ONLY when Status = SURVIVES.
# The user reviews the ledger (as domain owner) before any pixel is drawn.
# No claim goes on screen without a SURVIVES row in the ledger.
# Update the ledger BEFORE writing the code.
#
# CONFIDENCE TAGS (required on every Claim Ledger row):
# 🟢 TEXTBOOK   — grounded in a citeable standard (API RP, SPE, IEEE, OEM manual)
# 🟡 OUR-CODE   — number comes from FAULT_PROFILES/RESOLUTION_OPTIONS/FAULT_PHYSICS;
#                  grep-verifiable; "true to our system" (system values may still need SME review)
# 🔴 NEEDS-EXPERT — plausible but not authoritatively sourced; my estimate;
#                   operationally specific (field economics, lease practices).
#                   🔴 claims MAY NOT ship as hard facts. They must be:
#                   (a) softened to a defensible range, OR
#                   (b) verified by an O&G SME before use, OR
#                   (c) cut entirely.

## Engineering Decision-Making
- State technical positions clearly and defend them. Do not immediately defer or present excessive alternatives when a clear best answer exists.
- If the user pushes back or asks a clarifying question, it is NOT a signal to abandon the recommendation — evaluate the substance of the pushback and either update the position with reasoning or hold it.
- Avoid listing 3+ options when one option is clearly superior. Pick the best option, explain why briefly, and proceed.

## Planning Efficiency
- Use ASCII wireframes, tables, and short structured lists to validate designs before writing code. These are cheaper to revise than 500-line implementations.
- Do not enter Act Mode and write large files until the layout and feature set have been explicitly approved by the user.
- When a plan spans multiple files, write a short implementation checklist and get agreement on the sequence before starting.

## O&G Domain Realism
- All scenarios, sensor ranges, fault physics, and unstructured data (mud logs, lab reports, shift notes) must be physically realistic for upstream oil & gas operations (drilling, completion, production).
- Do not invent sensor values, fluid properties, or operational procedures that an O&G professional would immediately recognize as wrong.
- Cite the relevant standard or physical principle when introducing a new fault scenario or sensor interpretation.

## Code Quality
- The current stack is FastAPI (Python) + vanilla HTML/JS. Vue.js via CDN is approved for the UI layer.
- Do not introduce build-pipeline dependencies (npm, webpack, React JSX) into the fault-trigger-ui container without explicit discussion and approval.
- Preserve the existing XGBoost health score models (*.ubj files). They are correct and validated. Do not retrain without an explicit reason.
- The existing /api/* endpoints must remain backward-compatible unless we explicitly agree to break them.

## Deployment Constraint
- `terraform/gke.tf` must NOT be applied — it would destroy the live cluster.
- All demo changes go into gke/fault-trigger-ui/index.html and gke/fault-trigger-ui/app.py.
- After changes: docker build → docker push → kubectl rollout restart.
- Note: This SSH remote does not have a browser, so the `browser_action` tool should not be used.

## ── AUTOMATIC SESSION STARTUP ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# At the start of EVERY new conversation — before responding to any user request,
# before reading any file, before writing any code — automatically run all four
# of these commands and report the results to the user. Do not wait to be asked.
#
# This is not optional. It is the first action in every session.
#
execute_on_start:
  - kubectl get pods -n gdc-pm --no-headers
  - kubectl get deployment ollama -n gdc-pm -o jsonpath='{.spec.replicas}'; echo ""
  - curl -s http://gdc-pm.bdau.io/api/mlops/status | python3 -c "import sys,json;d=json.load(sys.stdin);print('ollama_online:',d.get('ollama_online'),'model:',d.get('ollama_model'))"
  - kubectl exec -n gdc-pm deployment/alloydb-omni -- psql -U postgres -d grid_reliability -c "SELECT COUNT(*) FROM field_intel; SELECT COUNT(*) FROM rag_documents;"
#
# After running these commands:
# 1. Compare each result against NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md "Expected results when healthy"
# 2. Report any discrepancy to the user immediately
# 3. If anything doesn't match: ask the user to confirm the correct state before proceeding
# 4. Do NOT write any code until all 4 commands have been run and their output reported
#
# Then read BOTH of the following documents before writing any code:
# 1. gdc-pm/docs/NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md — operational state (cluster, git head, next 2-3 tasks)
# 2. gdc-pm/docs/DEMO_MASTER.md — AUTHORITATIVE spec for what we are building
#    (H1/H2/H3 design, UI patterns, physics, agentic elements, implementation order)
# Reading DEMO_MASTER.md is mandatory. It takes precedence over all other docs.
# Also read the last 3 entries of docs/SESSION_LOG.md for recent decision context.
#

## Integrity — No Silent Lies
- Any code that DISPLAYS value X but ACTUALLY has value Y is an integrity violation that must be documented and fixed, never left as an acceptable design pattern.
- Examples of violations: hardcoded display labels that don't reflect actual state, health checks that always return green, model names shown in UI that aren't actually running.
- If you create or encounter such a mismatch, add it immediately to the "Known Integrity State" table in NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md with a fix deadline.

## Atomic Fix Rule
- Implement ONE fix per session if possible. Never more than THREE related fixes.
- After each fix: verify it works in the live cluster (kubectl, curl the API, or check logs), THEN write the next fix.
- A fix is NOT complete until it is deployed and verified. "Code written" ≠ "done."

## Token Budget Discipline
- After completing and verifying 2-3 fixes: update NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md and explicitly ask the user whether to continue or end cleanly.
- At 80% of the context window: stop all implementation. Document only. Do not start any new code.
- At 95%: hard stop. Write the handoff and end.
- Never start a fix that cannot be completed and verified within the remaining context budget.
- The task_progress checklist in each tool call must reflect true state — mark nothing complete until it is deployed and verified.

## Token Conservation — Large File Edits
The two primary files in this project are extremely large and their full content is returned into the context window after every edit:
- `gke/fault-trigger-ui/index.html`: ~3,500 lines (~150K tokens per return)
- `gke/fault-trigger-ui/app.py`: ~5,100 lines (~250K tokens per return)

**Mandatory practices to contain token spend:**
1. **Batch all edits to the same file into a single `replace_in_file` call** using multiple SEARCH/REPLACE blocks in one tool invocation. Never make sequential single-block edits to the same file — each invocation returns the full file, multiplying cost proportionally to the number of calls.
2. **Never read entire large files cold.** Use `grep -n` to locate exact line numbers first, then `read_file` with `start_line`/`end_line` to read only the targeted section. Never call `read_file` on app.py or index.html without line bounds unless the entire file content is genuinely needed.
3. **Two edits = two full-file returns = ~400-500K tokens consumed.** Before making any edit, ask: can this be combined with the next planned edit? If yes, defer until all planned edits are ready, then execute as one batched call.
4. **Parallel `replace_in_file` calls to the same file are permitted** but must target non-overlapping sections. Verify correctness with `grep -n` after, not by re-reading the full final_file_content output from the tool response.

## Session Handoff — Three-Document Architecture

There are now THREE documentation files with strictly defined roles. Do NOT conflate them.

### 1. `docs/DEMO_MASTER.md` — Permanent Spec (update only on design changes)
Contains: value proposition, H1/H2/H3 specs, UI patterns, physics, agentic elements, implementation order. Updated ONLY when an explicit design decision changes. Never cleared or rewritten wholesale.

### 2. `docs/NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md` — Operational State (~40 lines max)
Contains ONLY: cluster state, mandatory startup commands, git head, image digest, next 2-3 specific tasks. **Rewrite this file every session.** Keep it short. It is NOT a narrative — it is an operational checklist.

### 3. `docs/SESSION_LOG.md` — Append-Only History
One paragraph per session. **Never delete or overwrite entries.** Append a new entry at the top each session. New sessions read only the last 3-5 entries.

### Pre-Handoff Checklist (run before writing handoff)
1. `git status` — if uncommitted changes, commit them first
2. Verify all work is deployed and live (`kubectl get pods`, `curl` the API endpoint)
3. Run the 4 mandatory startup commands and record actual output in NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md
4. Check if any DEMO_MASTER.md design decisions were changed this session — if yes, update that file
5. Append this session's summary to SESSION_LOG.md (one paragraph: decisions made, code committed, key rejections, next task)

### NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md Format (the new compact format)
```
# Next Session Prompt — GDC Edge AI Demo (Operational State)
Date / git head / image digest / branch

## STEP 1: Run These Four Commands First
[4 commands + expected output]

## STEP 2: Read DEMO_MASTER.md
[cat command]

## STEP 3: Next Implementation Task
[specific tasks from DEMO_MASTER.md §12 Implementation Order]

## Constraints (Permanent)
[short list]
```

- **Trigger phrase:** When the user says **"wrap session"**, immediately: (1) run the Pre-Handoff Checklist, (2) update NEXT_SESSION_PROMPT.md in compact format, (3) append to SESSION_LOG.md, (4) update DEMO_MASTER.md if design changed, (5) commit all three docs, (6) confirm with one-paragraph summary.
